\begin{abstract}
We present a self-contained, machine-checked development of the algebraic and analytic core of lattice-based cryptography built on the Learning With Errors (LWE) problem. The development is organized around four pillars. First, the \emph{algebra of rerandomization}: over a prime field $\Z_p$, affine maps $x \mapsto ax + b$ with $a \neq 0$ are bijections, yielding the summation invariance $\sum_x f(ax+b) = \sum_x f(x)$ that makes wrong guesses in the search-to-decision reduction perfectly uniform. Second, \emph{decryption correctness}: accumulated noise from $m$ samples is bounded by $mB$, and Regev's rounding decryption recovers a bit exactly whenever the total noise is below $q/4$; the Dual-Regev round-trip satisfies the residual identity $\Dec(\Enc(\mu, r)) = \mu + \sum_i r_i e_i$. Third, the \emph{hybrid reduction calculus}: a telescoping triangle inequality bounds the end-to-end distinguishing advantage by a sum of neighboring gaps, and a dual pigeonhole principle extracts a single large gap, giving the linear advantage-loss factor of the search-to-decision and CPA reductions. Fourth, a \emph{distribution-level abstraction}: the same telescope holds for total variation distance over arbitrary distributions, showing that the reduction is fundamentally measure-theoretic; it requires no ring structure, and we use it to subsume non-commutative module-LWE and NTRU under one framework, with a data-processing (contraction) inequality guaranteeing robustness under linear post-processing. All stated results are formally verified and depend only on the standard foundational axioms.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The advent of Shor's algorithm rendered factoring- and discrete-log-based cryptography vulnerable to quantum attack, prompting a global migration toward \emph{post-quantum} schemes. The dominant family is lattice-based, and at its heart lies the \emph{Learning With Errors} problem introduced by Regev (2005). LWE enjoys a remarkable worst-case to average-case connection: solving random LWE instances is at least as hard as solving worst-case instances of approximate lattice problems such as GapSVP, a hardness believed to hold against quantum adversaries.

This paper formalizes the structural backbone of the LWE security argument. Rather than reproducing a particular reduction in full probabilistic detail, we isolate and verify the load-bearing mathematical facts on which every concrete LWE-based reduction depends. Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A verified treatment of affine bijections over $\Z_p$ and the summation invariance underlying rerandomization (\S\ref{sec:affine}).
  \item Verified noise-accumulation bounds and Regev rounding-decryption correctness, including modulus switching (\S\ref{sec:noise}).
  \item The Dual-Regev decryption identity and its perfect-correctness corollary (\S\ref{sec:dualregev}).
  \item The hybrid telescope and averaging lemmas, assembled into search-to-decision and end-to-end CPA bounds (\S\ref{sec:hybrid}).
  \item A distribution-level (total-variation) telescope and its application to non-commutative module-LWE and NTRU, plus a data-processing contraction inequality (\S\ref{sec:dist}).
  \item The Ring-LWE specialization via $\Z$-linearity of ring multiplication (\S\ref{sec:ring}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Preliminaries and Notation}

We work over the ring $\Zq = \Z/q\Z$ of integers modulo $q$. When $q = p$ is prime, $\Z_p$ is a field. Vectors $a, s \in \Zq^n$ have dot product $\inner{a}{s} = \sum_j a_j s_j$. We abstract a small-error distribution as a bound $|e| \le B$ on representatives.

\begin{definition}[LWE distribution]
For a secret $s \in \Zq^n$, an LWE sample is the pair $(a,b)$ where $a \leftarrow \Zq^n$ is uniform and $b = \inner{a}{s} + e \bmod q$ with $e$ a small error. \emph{Search-LWE} asks to recover $s$; \emph{decision-LWE} asks to distinguish such samples from uniform pairs.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total variation distance]
For probability mass functions $\mu, \nu$ on a finite set $\Omega$,
\[ \TVD(\mu, \nu) = \tfrac12 \sum_{\omega \in \Omega} |\mu(\omega) - \nu(\omega)|. \]
$\TVD$ is a metric and in particular satisfies the triangle inequality.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Distinguishing advantage]
For an adversary $\mathcal{A}$ and experiments producing $D_0, D_1$, $\Adv(\mathcal{A}) = |\Pr[\mathcal{A}(D_0)=1] - \Pr[\mathcal{A}(D_1)=1]| \le \TVD(D_0, D_1)$.
\end{definition}

\section{The Algebra of Rerandomization}\label{sec:affine}

The search-to-decision reduction recovers the secret one coordinate at a time, applying an affine transformation to the public component of each sample. Correctness hinges on these transformations being measure-preserving, which over a prime field is exact.

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{ZMod.mul\_left\_bijective\_of\_prime}]
Let $p$ be prime and $a \in \Z_p$ with $a \neq 0$. Then $x \mapsto a x$ is a bijection of $\Z_p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $p$ is prime, $\Z_p$ is a field, so nonzero $a$ is a unit and $x \mapsto ax$ is injective; injectivity on a finite type implies bijectivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{ZMod.affine\_bijective}]
For prime $p$ and $a, b \in \Z_p$ with $a \neq 0$, the map $x \mapsto a x + b$ is a bijection of $\Z_p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compose the bijection $x \mapsto ax$ with the translation $y \mapsto y + b$, a bijection of the additive group.
\end{proof}

We package this as an equivalence $\Z_p \simeq \Z_p$ whose inverse is affine: if $f(x) = ax+b$ then $f^{-1}(y) = a^{-1}(y-b)$. Affine maps are closed under composition:
\[ (x \mapsto a_1 x + b_1) \circ (x \mapsto a_2 x + b_2) = \big(x \mapsto (a_1 a_2)x + (a_1 b_2 + b_1)\big). \]

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{ZMod.affine\_image\_univ}]
For prime $p$ and $a \neq 0$, $\{ax + b : x \in \Z_p\} = \Z_p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{ZMod.sum\_affine\_eq}]
For prime $p$, $a \neq 0$, and any $f : \Z_p \to \R$,
\[ \sum_{x \in \Z_p} f(ax + b) = \sum_{x \in \Z_p} f(x). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Reindex along the bijection; a bijective reparametrization leaves a finite sum invariant.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Cryptographic reading.} For an incorrect secret guess, rerandomized samples are distributed as $f(ax+b)$ for uniform $x$, which equals the uniform expectation; the distinguisher gains no signal. Only a correct guess breaks the symmetry.

\section{Noise Accumulation and Rounding Decryption}\label{sec:noise}

\subsection{Noise accumulation}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{noise\_accumulation\_bound}]
If $|e_i| \le B$ for all $i$, then $\big|\sum_{i} e_i\big| \le m B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\big|\sum_i e_i\big| \le \sum_i |e_i| \le \sum_i B = mB$.
\end{proof}

The same argument gives a subset version $\big|\sum_{i\in S} e_i\big| \le |S|\,B$ (\texttt{noise\_accumulation\_subset\_bound}) and a real-valued version with bound $mB$ (\texttt{noise\_accumulation\_bound\_real}).

\subsection{Rounding decryption}

A bit $\mu \in \{0,1\}$ is encoded as $\mu \cdot (q/2)$; decryption tests which half of $[0,q)$ the noisy value occupies.

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{regev\_rounding\_bit0}]
If $|e| < q/4$ then $-q/4 < e < q/4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{regev\_rounding\_bit1}]
If $q > 0$ and $|e| < q/4$ then $\frac{q}{4} < \frac{q}{2} + e < \frac{3q}{4}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{encoding\_separation}]
If $|e|, |e'| < q/4$ and $q > 0$ then $0 < q/2 - |e| - |e'|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{regev\_encryption\_rounding\_correctness}]
For $q > 0$, $|e| < q/4$ and any $\mu$, $\big| \mu(q/2) + e - \mu(q/2)\big| < q/4$, so decryption recovers $\mu$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Modulus switching}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{combined\_noise\_after\_switching}]
If $|e_{\mathrm{lwe}}| \le B$ and each $|r_i| \le \delta$, then $\big| e_{\mathrm{lwe}} + \sum_{i=1}^n r_i\big| \le B + n\delta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{decryption\_correct\_after\_switching}]
Under the above hypotheses, if $B + n\delta < q/4$, then the combined noise is below $q/4$ and decryption remains correct.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Amplification and the modulus--noise tradeoff}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{advantage\_amplification}]
For $0 \le p \le 1$ and $k \ge 1$, $p \le 1 - (1-p)^k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{modulus\_noise\_tradeoff}]
For $q > 0$ and any $\alpha$ with $2\sqrt{n}/q \le \alpha$, we have $2\sqrt{n} \le \alpha q$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The Dual-Regev Scheme}\label{sec:dualregev}

We model Dual-Regev abstractly: a secret key $sk$, a public key with $p_i = \inner{A_i}{s} + e_i$ (a well-formedness predicate), encryption $\Enc(pk, \mu, r)$ with randomness $r = (r_i)$, and decryption $\Dec(sk, \cdot)$ computing $v - \inner{u}{s}$.

\begin{theorem}[Decryption identity, \texttt{dualRegev\_decrypt\_encrypt\_eq}]
For a well-formed public key with errors $e = (e_i)$,
\[ \Dec\big(sk, \Enc(pk, \mu, r)\big) = \mu + \sum_{i=1}^{m} r_i\, e_i. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Unfold definitions and substitute $p_i = \inner{A_i}{s} + e_i$ into decryption. The $\inner{\cdot}{s}$ terms cancel by bilinearity and a reindexing of the double sum, leaving $\mu$ plus the residual $\sum_i r_i e_i$. This is the adjoint cancellation $\inner{Tx}{y} = \inner{x}{T^\top y}$ specialized to the dot product.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Perfect correctness, \texttt{dualRegev\_decrypt\_correct\_zero\_noise}]
If all errors are zero then $\Dec(sk, \Enc(pk, \mu, r)) = \mu$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The Hybrid Reduction Calculus}\label{sec:hybrid}

\subsection{Telescope and averaging}

\begin{theorem}[Hybrid telescope, \texttt{hybrid\_telescope\_bound}]
For $P : \{0, \dots, k+1\} \to \R$,
\[ \big| P_0 - P_{k+1} \big| \le \sum_{i=0}^{k} \big| P_i - P_{i+1} \big|. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $k$, with step $|P_0 - P_{k+2}| \le |P_0 - P_{k+1}| + |P_{k+1} - P_{k+2}|$ and the inductive hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hybrid averaging, \texttt{hybrid\_averaging}]
If $\varepsilon > 0$ and $\varepsilon \le |P_0 - P_{k+1}|$, then some index $i$ satisfies $\frac{\varepsilon}{k+1} \le |P_i - P_{i+1}|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Contrapositive: if all gaps were $< \varepsilon/(k+1)$ their sum is $< \varepsilon$, contradicting the telescope bound.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Search-to-decision}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{search\_to\_decision\_advantage\_bound}]
For $n > 0$, if $\delta \le \sum_{i=1}^{n} c_i$, then some $c_i \ge \delta/n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{search\_from\_decision\_coordinate}]
For $n > 0$, $\varepsilon > 0$, hybrid probabilities $H$ with $\varepsilon \le |H_0 - H_n|$, and coordinate advantages bounding each gap ($|H_i - H_{i+1}| \le c_i$), there exists $i$ with $\varepsilon/n \le c_i$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{CPA security and end-to-end composition}

\begin{theorem}[CPA from LWE, \texttt{dualRegev\_cpa\_security\_of\_lwe}]
For nonnegative advantages, if $\mathrm{adv}_{\mathrm{LWE}} \ge \mathrm{adv}_{\mathrm{CPA}} - \varepsilon_{\mathrm{corr}}$, then $\mathrm{adv}_{\mathrm{CPA}} \le \mathrm{adv}_{\mathrm{LWE}} + \varepsilon_{\mathrm{corr}}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[End-to-end composition, \texttt{endToEnd\_security\_composition}]
For $n > 0$ and nonnegative advantages, if $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{decision}} \le n\,\varepsilon_{\mathrm{search}}$ and $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{cpa}} \le \varepsilon_{\mathrm{decision}} + \varepsilon_{\mathrm{corr}}$, then $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{cpa}} \le n\,\varepsilon_{\mathrm{search}} + \varepsilon_{\mathrm{corr}}$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The Distribution-Level Abstraction}\label{sec:dist}

\begin{theorem}[TVD telescope, \texttt{hybrid\_telescope\_tvd}]
For a finite type $\Omega$ and $H : \{0, \dots, n\} \to \mathrm{PMF}(\Omega)$,
\[ \TVD(H_0, H_n) \le \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \TVD(H_i, H_{i+1}). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction using the triangle inequality for $\TVD$, mirroring the real-valued telescope.
\end{proof}

\noindent The ring structure enters only when bounding individual neighboring gaps; the quantitative skeleton is measure-theoretic.

\subsection{Non-commutative module-LWE and NTRU}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{NoncommModuleLWEParams}]
Over a (possibly non-commutative) ring $R$, with left $R$-modules $M$ (secrets) and $N$ (samples): a sample count, secret distribution on $M$, error distribution on $N$, left-linear action map $T : M \to_R N$, and base distribution on $N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Advantages]
The one-step advantage is $\TVD(T_*\mu_s, \mu_{\mathrm{base}})$; the decision advantage is $\text{sampleCount} \cdot \text{oneStepAdvantage}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{decisionAdvantage\_le\_mul}]
$\text{decisionAdvantage} \le \text{sampleCount} \cdot \text{oneStepAdvantage}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{NTRUInstance}]
An NTRU-style system over $R$: a left-linear public map $M \to_R N$, secret distribution, noise distribution, sample count, and uniform reference distribution; \texttt{NTRUInstance.toParams} repackages it as \texttt{NoncommModuleLWEParams}.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{ntru\_instantiates\_noncomm\_module\_framework}]
Every NTRU instance arises as a \texttt{NoncommModuleLWEParams} with matching action map, secret distribution, and sample count.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{ntru\_decision\_reduction}]
For any NTRU instance $P$, $\text{decisionAdvantage}(P.\mathrm{toParams}) \le P.\mathrm{samples} \cdot \text{oneStepAdvantage}(P.\mathrm{toParams})$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Data-processing / contraction}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{quotient\_map\_tvd\_bound\_noncomm}]
For a left-linear $\varphi : M \to_R N$ and distributions $\mu, \nu$, $\TVD(\varphi_*\mu, \varphi_*\nu) \le \TVD(\mu, \nu)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{tvd\_map\_map\_le}]
For $f : \alpha \to \beta$, $g : \beta \to \gamma$ and distributions $\mu, \nu$, $\TVD((g\circ f)_*\mu, (g\circ f)_*\nu) \le \TVD(\mu, \nu)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The Ring-LWE Specialization}\label{sec:ring}

\begin{theorem}[Ring multiplication is $\Z$-linear, \texttt{ring\_mult\_is\_linear\_on\_coeffs}]
In any commutative ring $R$ that is a $\Z$-module, for fixed $a \in R$ the map $s \mapsto a\cdot s$ is $\Z$-linear.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Additivity is distributivity; $\Z$-homogeneity follows by induction over integer scalars using $a\cdot(k\,s) = k\,(a\cdot s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Advantage transport, \texttt{ringLWE\_advantage\_transport}]
If $\mathrm{adv}_{\mathrm{Ring}} \le \mathrm{adv}_{\mathrm{coeff}}$, the bound transfers verbatim.
\end{theorem}

Because $s \mapsto a\cdot s$ is linear, the Ring-LWE decryption identity has exactly the residual shape of the Dual-Regev identity, with matrix--vector adjoint cancellation replaced by ring-multiplication cancellation. The noise-smudging bound (\texttt{noise\_smudging\_bound}): if $\text{stat\_dist} \le \text{original\_noise}/\text{smudging\_noise}$ with smudging noise positive, the statistical distance is controlled by their ratio.

\section{Discussion}

The formalization separates concerns. The quantitative skeleton of every LWE reduction --- telescope plus pigeonhole --- is purely metric and holds for total variation distance over arbitrary distributions. The algebraic content enters only in two places: that wrong guesses produce uniform samples (the affine bijection), and that decryption cancels secret-dependent terms (the adjoint identity). Robustness under linear post-processing is automatic from the data-processing inequality. A single scalar threshold ``total noise $< q/4$'' governs decryption correctness across variants, making parameter selection a transparent inequality.

\section{Future Directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Unified residual functor]
A single $R$-module-level lemma, parameterized by an adjoint bilinear pairing, yields both the Dual-Regev and Ring-LWE decryption identities as specializations (pairing $=$ dot product, resp.\ ring multiplication via the regular representation). Both are instances of $\inner{Tx}{y} = \inner{x}{T^\top y}$, so cancellation is functorial in the pairing.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Non-field rings break naive search-to-decision]
Over $\Zq$ with $q$ composite, $\sum_x f(ax+b) = \sum_x f(x)$ fails for some non-unit $a \neq 0$; the unit hypothesis is necessary. When $a$ is a zero divisor, $ax$ ranges only over the ideal $(a)$, so uniformity is not preserved. A counterexample at $q = 6$, $a = 2$ pins the boundary and is a finite, decidable claim.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Tight noise budget across all three schemes]
A single inequality $B_{\text{total}} < q/4$ --- with $B_{\text{total}} = X_1 + mEX$ in matrix LWE and $B_{\text{total}} = \|e\|\,\|r\| + \|e_1\| + \|s\|\,\|e_0\|$ in Ring-LWE --- is both necessary and sufficient for one-bit decryption correctness, and tight.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a formally verified account of the structural core of LWE-based cryptography: the affine-bijection algebra powering rerandomization, the noise and rounding inequalities guaranteeing decryption, the Dual-Regev residual identity, the hybrid telescope and pigeonhole quantifying the reduction, the distribution-level abstraction revealing its measure-theoretic nature, and the Ring-LWE specialization via linearity of ring multiplication. The chain from worst-case lattice hardness to a quantum-resistant scheme rests, at bottom, on the triangle inequality.

